<p>The vLLM Bench failure is dramatic: it achieves 14.6% of its target. But the more insidious problem is the latency data it returns — those missed tokens accumulate as client-side backpressure, and the TTFT measurements include all of that waiting time. You're not measuring the model. You're measuring the Python event loop.</p>

<h3>Why this matters for infra decisions</h3>
<p>The paper's central observation: "what is frequently diagnosed as an infrastructural failure is, in reality, a measurement bias induced by the observer itself." Teams upgrade hardware, retune serving configs, or add more GPUs — not because the model is slow, but because their benchmark said it was.</p>
<ul>
  <li>At 1,000 QPS: vLLM Bench processed 75,574 tokens total vs Inference Perf's 545,733 — a <strong>7.2× discrepancy</strong></li>
  <li>At 5,000 QPS: vLLM Bench and Inference X hit timeout failures and had to be excluded from results entirely</li>
  <li>At 5,000 QPS: Inference Perf processed 2.6M tokens, outperforming the next best multi-process tool (AI Perf) by 24%</li>
</ul>
