<section id="s-queue" data-code="queue">
<div class="section-no">02 — Python GIL &amp; the Queue That Never Empties</div>
<h2>Formalizing the bottleneck with queueing theory</h2>
<p>Why does single-process asyncio fail at scale? The Python Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) allows only one thread to execute bytecode at a time. An asyncio event loop runs on a single CPU core — and when the incoming token rate exceeds what that core can process, you get a queue.</p>
<p>The paper models the benchmarking client as an <strong>M/G/1 queue</strong>. The key variables:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>λ</strong> — arrival rate of tokens from the server across all concurrent streams</li>
  <li><strong>μ</strong> — service rate of the Python event loop (tokens it can deserialize and record per second)</li>
  <li><strong>ρ = λ/μ</strong> — utilization factor of the event loop</li>
</ul>
<p>The Pollaczek-Khinchine formula gives the expected client-side wait time:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">M/G/1 queue — Pollaczek-Khinchine mean wait time</div>
  <span class="math-var">W<sub>q</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">λ · E[S²]</span> / (2 · (1 − <span class="math-var">ρ</span>))<br>
  <span class="math-comment">where E[S²] is the second moment of event loop service time, ρ = λ/μ</span>
</div>
<p>The catastrophic term is <strong>(1 − ρ)</strong> in the denominator. As QPS scales toward production loads, λ approaches μ, ρ approaches 1, and W<sub>q</sub> approaches <strong>infinity</strong>. The event loop cannot drain faster than tokens arrive, so tokens wait arbitrarily long to be recorded — and that wait time shows up as inflated TTFT.</p>

<div class="vp">
