    <div class="queue-readout">
      <div class="queue-stat">
        <span class="queue-stat-label">Utilization ρ</span>
        <span class="queue-stat-value" id="q-rho">0.85</span>
      </div>
      <div class="queue-stat">
        <span class="queue-stat-label">Normalized W<sub>q</sub></span>
        <span class="queue-stat-value" id="q-wq">5.67×</span>
      </div>
      <div class="queue-stat">
        <span class="queue-stat-label">Status</span>
        <span class="queue-stat-value" id="q-status">Degrading</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The fix the paper proposes: distribute the load across <strong>k isolated processes</strong>. Each process only sees λ/k token arrivals, so the per-process utilization factor becomes ρ<sub>k</sub> = λ/(kμ) — well below 1 regardless of how high QPS climbs. The wait time stays negligible because no single event loop is overwhelmed.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Multi-process utilization — load distributed across k workers</div>
  <span class="math-highlight">ρ<sub>k</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">λ</span> / (<span class="math-highlight">k</span> · <span class="math-var">μ</span>) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># each process only handles 1/k of total traffic</span><br>
  As k ↑, ρ<sub>k</sub> ↓ &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># utilization stays safely below 1</span>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-fix" data-code="fix">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Fix: Multi-Process Architecture</div>
<h2>Distributing load to bypass the GIL</h2>
<p>The paper proposes a distributed evaluation framework, instantiated as <strong>Inference Perf</strong> (open-source, Kubernetes SIGs). The architecture spawns m isolated worker processes matching available vCPUs. Each worker manages its own asyncio event loop with at most n concurrent coroutines. A central orchestration process handles load generation and metrics aggregation — the workers never share a GIL.</p>

