<h3>Dynamic concurrency regulation</h3>
<p>Inference Perf exposes <code>max_workers</code> and <code>worker_max_concurrency</code> parameters, but the framework automatically determines optimal values based on the target load and available vCPUs — keeping each worker's utilization well below its saturation threshold. The paper also introduces two internal fidelity metrics to validate that the benchmark itself is behaving correctly:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scheduling Precision:</strong> median, P90, P99 delay between intended dispatch time and actual transmission must stay under 1ms</li>
  <li><strong>Dispatch Throughput:</strong> actual QPS transmitted by the load generator, separate from response-based QPS</li>
</ul>
<p>The cross-paradigm validation is noteworthy: Go's k6 (native goroutines, no GIL) and Inference Perf both successfully sustained 5,000 QPS. Python-based tools including MLPerf's C++ core failed — the C++ core handles HTTP, but the Python bindings for request processing reintroduce the GIL bottleneck at high concurrency. The fix is not "use a faster language" but "distribute load across isolated Python processes."</p>
</section>

<section id="s-ntpot" data-code="ntpot">
<div class="section-no">04 — NTPOT: A Metric That Doesn't Lie</div>
<h2>Normalizing latency across sequence lengths</h2>
<p>Even with an accurate measurement tool, standard latency metrics have a fundamental comparison problem: TTFT and TPOT scale with input and output sequence length. A 10,000-token RAG query will always look worse than a 50-token chat turn on the same hardware — not because the model is degrading, but because there's more work to do. You can't compare two benchmarks run with different datasets.</p>
<p>The paper formalizes <strong>Normalized Time Per Output Token (NTPOT)</strong>, a composite metric that divides end-to-end latency by the number of output tokens generated:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">NTPOT — basic form</div>
  <span class="math-var">NTPOT<sub>i</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">L<sub>i</sub></span> / <span class="math-var">N<sub>i</sub></span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">L_i = total end-to-end latency for request i, N_i = output tokens generated</span>
</div>

<p>Expanding to expose the underlying serving phases:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">NTPOT — expanded form (all three latency phases visible)</div>
  <span class="math-var">NTPOT<sub>i</sub></span> = (<span class="math-highlight">T<sub>queue</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">I<sub>i</sub></span> · <span class="math-var">T<sub>prefill</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">N<sub>i</sub></span> · <span class="math-var">T<sub>decode</sub></span>) / <span class="math-var">N<sub>i</sub></span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">T_queue = server-side wait, I_i = input length, T_prefill = per-token prefill cost, T_decode = per-token decode cost</span>
</div>

<h3>Why pure TPOT is misleading at saturation</h3>
<p>In a heavily loaded system, pure decode TPOT often stays relatively flat even as the system degrades. The bottleneck shows up first in queue time and prefill compute — which TPOT ignores entirely, since it only measures the decode phase. NTPOT captures all three terms and divides them by output length, so a spike in p99 NTPOT directly signals that scheduling capacity is saturated, even when TPOT looks fine.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">TPOT vs NTPOT at Increasing Load</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">canvas — TPOT misleads where NTPOT reveals</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="ntpot-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Conceptual illustration. At high load TPOT stays flat; NTPOT spikes as queue and prefill costs compound.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>NTPOT also solves the RAG problem: a request with a 10,000-token prefix has a massive T<sub>prefill</sub> term, but that term is divided by N<sub>i</sub> output tokens, so the metric reflects the true per-output-token cost the end user waits for — not an arbitrary absolute latency. This makes cross-dataset and cross-model comparisons meaningful.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Smoking Gun: 58 Seconds of Fake Latency</div>
<h2>Empirical proof at 1,000 QPS</h2>
<p>The paper's most striking experiment uses a simulator (<code>llm-d-inference-sim</code>) that returns tokens with near-zero server-side latency. Since the server adds essentially nothing, any latency recorded by the benchmark is <em>pure measurement overhead</em> — the benchmark lying about itself.</p>
<p>All tests ran on a c4-standard-144 instance (144 vCPU Intel 6th Gen Xeon) with fixed-length inputs of 320 tokens and outputs of 200 tokens per request, eliminating hardware as a variable.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
