<p>The cross-paradigm test against native HTTP load generators (k6/Go, Locust/Python, MLPerf/C++) at 5,000 QPS is particularly illuminating. MLPerf has a C++ execution core but failed at 1,944 QPS — because its Python request-processing bindings reintroduce the GIL for the token recording step. Go's k6 and Inference Perf were the only tools to sustain 5,000 QPS. The lesson: Python can handle production-scale benchmarking if and only if load is distributed across isolated processes that each maintain a low utilization factor.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-pitfalls" data-code="pitfalls">
<div class="section-no">06 — Reproducibility Pitfalls</div>
<h2>Three more ways your benchmark is wrong</h2>
<p>Even with a multi-process tool, the paper identifies three additional sources of measurement bias that corrupt reproducibility across runs and across teams.</p>

<h3>1. Temperature settings change arithmetic intensity</h3>
<p>Setting temperature=0 enables greedy decoding, which eliminates probabilistic sampling and reduces the arithmetic intensity of each decode step. The same hardware processing the same 1,000 requests shows a <strong>21% throughput variance</strong> purely from this parameter:</p>

<div class="results-bars" id="temp-bars">
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Temp = 0.7 (realistic)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="82.4" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--cyan),#0891b2)"><span>5,573 tok/s</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Temp = 0 (greedy)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--amber),#d97706)"><span>6,767 tok/s</span></div></div>
  </div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono);margin-bottom:1.25rem">Benchmarks defaulting to Temp=0 report ~21% better throughput than real-world generative workloads deliver.</p>

<h3>2. Dataset processing discrepancies</h3>
<p>Two tools processing the identical ShareGPT dataset at 10 QPS for 3,000 requests processed wildly different amounts of input:</p>
<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Dataset</th><th>Input Tokens Processed</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Inference X</td><td class="mn">ShareGPT, 3000 req @ 10 QPS</td><td class="mn bad">702,412</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Inference Perf</td><td class="mn">ShareGPT, 3000 req @ 10 QPS</td><td class="mn best">1,052,100</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A 50% discrepancy from different prompt truncation strategies and random sampling of the dataset. Two teams running "the same benchmark" on "the same dataset" get results that can't be compared.</p>

<h3>3. Prefix caching invalidates TTFT comparisons</h3>
<p>Server-side prefix caching makes initial response times highly variable depending on request ordering — whether a request shares a common prefix with a recent request that's still cached. Two runs of the same benchmark can produce dramatically different TTFT distributions if request ordering differs. The fix: benchmark frameworks must explicitly report and control the cache hit ratio, and standardize prompt truncation limits across every run they publish.</p>
</section>

